# From rung 7 to the real thing

### Every rung, found again in tutors-mono-repo

Tutors Ladder, Summit, talk 1

---

## You have climbed seven rungs

- Rung 1 to 3: a generator, a static site, a layered workspace
- Rung 4 to 7: a reader, a proven suite, a guarded build, a shipped release
- Each one mirrors part of the real repository

This talk maps each rung to its upstream areas, then shows what the real repository adds that no rung covers.
All paths are at the pinned commit `2b96b69` of tutors-sdk/tutors-mono-repo.

---

## Rungs 1 to 3: the generator and its architecture

| Rung | Variant | Upstream areas |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 Seed | `rung-1-seed` | `packages/jsr/gen`, `packages/jsr/model`, `packages/jsr/tutors` |
| 2 Lite | `rung-2-lite` | `packages/jsr/tutors-lite` |
| 3 Layers | `rung-3-layers` | `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `packages/jsr/types`, the ports in `packages/jsr/course-rules`, `tests/architecture` |

Upstream adds: `packages/jsr/create` (the scaffolder and `tutors validate`), `packages/jsr/tutors-cli`, and
dependency-cruiser rules in `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`.

---

## Rungs 4 to 7: the four tracks

| Rung | Variant | Upstream areas |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 4 Reader | `rung-4-reader` | `apps/reader`, `packages/svelte/ui-*` |
| 5 Proven | `rung-5-proven` | `tests/`, `guides/testing`, `stryker.config.json` |
| 6 Guarded | `rung-6-guarded` | `packages/jsr/identity`, `packages/svelte/utils/rbac`, `docs/security`, `SECURITY.md` |
| 7 Shipped | `rung-7-shipped` | `.github/workflows`, `release/`, `Dockerfile`, tutors-release-harness |

Each variant's `RUNG.md` lists the exact files.

---

## Supabase: the data behind signed-in use

- `supabase/migrations/`: nine SQL migrations at the pinned commit, applied in name order
- `guides/MIGRATIONS.md`: expand and contract, so a rolling release never breaks the version still running;
  a merged migration is never edited, deleted or renamed
- `pnpm check:migrations` checks a new migration before CI does
- Browser packages reach personal data only through `@tutors/data-api` (`packages/svelte/data-api`), never
  through the database client

In anonymous mode (`PUBLIC_ANON_MODE=TRUE`) none of this runs. That is why the quick start needs no backend.

---

## Realtime and live

- `packages/jsr/realtime` (`@tutors/realtime`): the realtime port, with topics, the adapter contract and a
  budgeted gateway, and no broker imports
- `packages/svelte/realtime-supabase`: the one adapter that drives Supabase Realtime channels
- `apps/live`: the live classroom and presence app
- `tests/architecture/realtime-boundary.test.ts` fails on any other file that opens a channel

This is rung 3's ports and adapters, at full size.

---

## Catalogue, time and the validator

| App | What it is | Shares with the ladder |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `apps/catalogue` | course catalogue and discovery, with visit statistics | lists courses; its data comes from the backend |
| `apps/time` | student activity and time tracking, using `@tutors/tutors-time-lib` from `packages/jsr/time` | a JSR library, like rung 1's model |
| `apps/validator` | validates a published `tutors.json` (V020) or a private local ZIP | the same `course-rules` you ran in Orientation |

In anonymous mode the catalogue, live and time apps start but show no courses or students. That is
expected (CONTRIBUTING.md).

---

## JSR publishing

- `.github/workflows/jsr-publish.yml`: when a final release is published, every library in `packages/jsr`
  whose `deno.json` version JSR does not serve yet is published, dependencies first (Rule 0775)
- `deno publish` authenticates with GitHub's OIDC token, so no JSR token is stored, and JSR records Sigstore
  provenance
- That is how `jsr:@tutors/tutors-create validate`, which every course's standard `netlify.toml` runs,
  reaches lecturers

---

## The release harness

A separate repository, tutors-sdk/tutors-release-harness:

- runs a release candidate's images beside production's
- drives the same scripted traffic through both, normalises the noise and diffs everything observable
- **fails unless every difference is claimed** in `release/claims.yaml` by a changelog entry or a Rule
- verifies the cosign signature of every image it pulls from `quay.io/tutors-sdk/tutors-<app>`

Being separate means "it cannot be quietly weakened by the PR it is judging" (its README).

---

## The release itself

- `release/SOP.md`: twelve steps, each with an owner, an input and a done-when, organised around five Lean
  ideas
- `pnpm release:candidate X.Y.Z` runs steps 1, 2 and 5 to 7, then stops at step 8, the reviewer's glance,
  "because step 8 must stay human"
- Contributors own steps 3 and 4: the changelog line with its artefact hints, and the claims

Rung 7 built a small version of this. The real one has a harness on the other side.

---

## Service level objectives

- `release/slo.yaml`: indicative availability and latency targets per app, over 28 days
- `docs/slo.md` explains them: the reader is strictest, 99.5% not 5xx and 95% under one second
- Grafana alerts are generated from the file (`pnpm slo:alerts`); `pnpm check:slo` fails if they drift
- The harness judges each candidate against the stricter of these and its own floors (`slo/floors.yaml`)

---

## Supply chain: the OpenSSF Scorecard and friends

- `.github/workflows/scorecard.yml`: OpenSSF Scorecard weekly (Mondays 07:20 UTC), on pushes to `main` and
  on branch-protection changes; results go to code scanning and the public dashboard
- The README carries the Scorecard and OpenSSF Baseline badges
- `codeql.yml` and `zizmor.yml` scan the code and the workflows themselves
- Images are signed with cosign, keyless, from GitHub OIDC (`image-build.yml`)

Rung 6 starts this story; the real repository finishes it.

---

## Sizes, side by side

| | Ladder | tutors-mono-repo |
| --- | --- | --- |
| apps | one reader (rung 4) | 5 |
| packages | a few workspace packages, from rung 3 | 11 under `packages/jsr`, 21 under `packages/svelte` |
| workflows | the ones rung 7 adds | 17 |
| EARS Rules | the ones rung 5 writes | over 400 ids in 85 feature files |

You will not hold all of it in your head. You do not need to. You need the rung your change sits on.

---

## Summary

- Every rung has a home in the real repository, and you know its shape
- What is new at the Summit is mostly **scale** and **operation**: data, realtime, releases, SLOs
- The next talk: how to change it safely, with an agent, one rung at a time
